 ## Overview
-The Airlight Point Spread Function (PSF) models the angular distribution of scattered light in a participating medium, treating the medium as a blur kernel that modifies incident radiance [S1]. It approximates multiple scattering effects in real-time rendering by normalizing radiance into a function of the scattering angle $\gamma$, avoiding costly direct numerical simulation of the radiative transfer equation [S1]. This method builds on [PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering](1297-psf-based-multiple-scattering-rendering.md) and differs from [Narrow beam distribution method](9069-narrow-beam-distribution-method.md) and [PSF Modeling](21315-psf-modeling.md) by explicitly normalizing radiance by source intensity and distance to isolate the angular scattering profile [S1].
+The Airlight Point Spread Function (PSF) models the angular distribution of scattered light in a participating medium by treating the medium as a blur kernel that modifies incident radiance [S1]. It approximates multiple scattering effects in real-time rendering by normalizing radiance into a function of the scattering angle $\gamma$, avoiding costly direct numerical simulation of the radiative transfer equation [S1]. This method builds on [PSF-based Multiple Scattering Rendering](1297-psf-based-multiple-scattering-rendering.md) and differs from [Narrow beam distribution method](9069-narrow-beam-distribution-method.md) and [PSF Modeling](21315-psf-modeling.md) by explicitly normalizing radiance by source intensity and distance to isolate the angular scattering profile [S1].
 
